.TH ERROR 5
.SH NAME
lerror \- return an error
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B size[4] Rlerror tag[2] ecode[4]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Rlerror
is the reply to a request the server understood and refused.
There is no
.BR Tlerror ;
type 6 is reserved and illegal on the wire.
.B Rlerror
replaces whatever reply the request would otherwise have carried,
and its tag is the failing request's own.
.PP
9P2000.L's break from plain 9P is here:
.I ecode
is a number, not a string.
It is one of Linux's errno values, because that is what a Linux
client expects and Vectra's codec is a wire-compatibility layer over
it, not an endorsement of errno as an error model.
The names Vectra's servers actually produce, and their numbers:
.PP
.nf
EPERM 1     EACCES 13    ENFILE 23      ENOTEMPTY 39
ENOENT 2    EFAULT 14    EMFILE 24      ELOOP 40
ESRCH 3     EBUSY 16     ENOSPC 28      EPROTO 71
EINTR 4     EEXIST 17    ESPIPE 29      EOPNOTSUPP 95
EIO 5       EXDEV 18     EROFS 30       ETIMEDOUT 110
ENXIO 6     ENODEV 19    EMLINK 31      ECONNREFUSED 111
E2BIG 7     ENOTDIR 20   EPIPE 32
ENOEXEC 8   EISDIR 21    EDEADLK 35
EBADF 9     EINVAL 22    ENAMETOOLONG 36
ECHILD 10                ENOSYS 38
EAGAIN 11
ENOMEM 12
.fi
.PP
An unnamed number is still a legal
.IR ecode ;
it simply prints as a number rather than a name, both to a client
and in a kernel log.
.PP
This is distinct from a codec failure: a message whose bytes are
short, malformed, or of a type the server does not recognise has no
.B Rlerror
built for it, because the request was never understood well enough
to answer.
See
.IR intro (5).
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/vectra9/proto.odin
.br
.B sys/vectra9/errors.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (5),
.IR flush (5)
